            "text": "I need to write tests that cover lines 337-346 of `Queue._format`. The `_format` method is called by `__repr__` and `__str__`. Let me check what `__repr__` and `__str__` look like \u2014 they call `_format`.\n\nTo cover all branches:\n1. Line 337: `result = \"maxsize=%r\" % (self.maxsize,)` \u2014 always executes when `_format` is called.\n2. Line 338-339: `if getattr(self, \"_queue\", None): result += \" queue=%r\" % self._queue` \u2014 need a queue with items in `_queue` (non-empty). Also the branch where `_queue` is empty/falsy.\n3. Line 340-341: `if self._getters: result += \" getters[%s]\" % len(self._getters)` \u2014 need a getter waiting (a coroutine blocked on `get()` on an empty queue).\n4. Line 342-343: `if self._putters: result += \" putters[%s]\" % len(self._putters)` \u2014 need a putter waiting (a coroutine blocked on `put()` on a full queue).\n5. Line 344-345: `if self._unfinished_tasks: result += \" tasks=%s\" % self._unfinished_tasks` \u2014 need unfinished tasks (put items without calling `task_done`).\n\nSo I need to write an async test that:\n- Creates a `Queue(maxsize=1)`.\n- Puts an item (unfinished task count becomes 1).\n- Starts a getter coroutine blocked on `get()` (empty queue... wait, if we put an item first, the getter won't block). Let me plan carefully.\n\nPlan:\n- `q = Queue(maxsize=1)`\n- Put one item via `put_nowait` \u2014 this increments `_unfinished_tasks` to 1 and makes `_queue` non-empty. Covers lines 338-339 and 344-345.\n- Start a getter coroutine: `getter = q.get()` \u2014 since queue is non-empty, it would return immediately. Hmm, I need the getter to block. So the getter must be started when the queue is empty.\n\nLet me restructure:\n- `q = Queue(maxsize=1)`\n- Start getter coroutine first: `getter_future = q.get()` \u2014 queue is empty, so getter is added to `_getters`. Covers lines 340-341.\n- Then put an item: `q.put_nowait(\"x\")` \u2014 this resolves the getter, so `_getters` becomes empty. Hmm, that removes the getters branch.\n\nI need both getters and putters present simultaneously in `_format`. Getters wait when queue is empty; putters wait when queue is full. With maxsize=1, the queue can hold 1 item. Can we have both a getter and a putter waiting? If the queue is empty, a getter waits. If the queue is full, a putter waits. These are mutually exclusive states... unless we manipulate directly.\n\nWait \u2014 actually, let's think. With maxsize=1:\n- Queue empty: getter blocks.\n- Queue full (1 item): putter blocks.\n\nCan we have a getter blocked AND a putter blocked at the same time? The queue state is either empty or full (or has 1 item which is both non-empty and full). If queue has 1 item: getter would take it immediately (get_nowait succeeds), so getter doesn't block. If queue is empty: put_nowait succeeds, so putter doesn't block.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: when a getter is blocked and someone puts an item, the item is handed directly to the getter (via `_put` then waking the getter... let me recall the actual tornado implementation).\n\nLet me recall tornado's Queue implementation:\n\n```python\ndef put_nowait(self, item):\n    self._consume_expired()\n    if self._getters:\n        assert not self._queue, \"queue non-empty, why are getters waiting?\"\n        getter = self._getters.popleft()\n        self.__put_internal(item)\n        getter.set_result(self._get())\n    elif self.full():\n        raise QueueFull\n    else:\n        self.__put_internal(item)\n\ndef get_nowait(self):\n    self._consume_expired()\n    if self._putters:\n        assert self.full(), \"queue empty, why are putters waiting?\"\n        item, putter = self._putters.popleft()\n        self._get()\n        putter.set_result(None)\n        return item\n    ...\n```\n\nSo when a getter is waiting and we put, the item goes directly to the getter. When a putter is waiting and we get, the putter's item is taken.\n\nSo to have both getters and putters waiting simultaneously: \n- Start with empty queue, maxsize=1.\n- Start getter coroutine: `getter = q.get()` \u2014 blocks, `_getters` has 1 entry.\n- Now put an item with `put_nowait` \u2014 this resolves the getter directly. `_getters` empty again.\n\nAlternative: use `put()` (async) when queue is full:\n- `q = Queue(maxsize=1)`\n- `q.put_nowait(\"a\")` \u2014 queue full now, `_unfinished_tasks = 1`.\n- Start putter coroutine: `putter = q.put(\"b\")` \u2014 blocks since full, `_putters` has 1 entry.\n- Now start getter coroutine: `getter = q.get()` \u2014 queue is non-empty (has \"a\"), so `get_nowait` path: `if self._putters:` \u2014 yes, putters waiting. It pops the putter, gets the item \"a\", resolves the putter. So `_putters` becomes empty and the getter gets \"a\" immediately.\n\nHmm, so with the standard flow, getters and putters can't both be waiting. Unless... we craft the state manually.\n\nActually wait \u2014 there's a scenario: expired putters/getters. `_consume_expired` removes putters/getters whose timeouts expired. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me think about whether we can have both. Consider maxsize=1:\n1. `q.put_nowait(\"a\")` \u2014 queue: [\"a\"], full.\n2. `putter = q.put(\"b\")` \u2014 putter waits. `_putters = [putter]`.\n3. Now the queue is full. A getter: `getter = q.get()` \u2014 `get_nowait` checks `if self._putters:` \u2014 pops putter, returns \"a\". So getter doesn't block.\n\nWhat if instead of a real getter, we directly append to `_getters`? That's hacky but works for coverage. But better to use public API.\n\nAlternative approach: use two separate `_format` calls at different times, or use multiple test functions. The requirement is to execute all missing lines and branches \u2014 not necessarily in a single test. So I can write multiple tests:\n\nTest 1: empty queue repr \u2014 covers line 337, and the false branches of 338, 340, 342, 344.\nTest 2: queue with items \u2014 covers 338-339 (true branch) and 344-345 (true branch, since put increments unfinished tasks).\nTest 3: getter waiting \u2014 covers 340-341 (true branch).\nTest 4: putter waiting \u2014 covers 342-343 (true branch).\n\nActually, can I combine 3 and 4? Let me think again about whether both getters and putters can be waiting simultaneously.\n\nScenario with maxsize=1:\n1. `q.put_nowait(\"a\")` \u2014 queue full.\n2. `putter = q.put(\"b\")` \u2014 putter blocks.\n3. `getter = q.get()` \u2014 pops putter, gets \"a\". Getter gets \"a\" immediately, putter resolved.\n\nWhat about maxsize=2?\n1. `q.put_nowait(\"a\")` \u2014 queue: [\"a\"].\n2. `putter = q.put(\"b\")` \u2014 queue not full (1 < 2), so put succeeds immediately. No putter waiting.\n\nHmm. What about timing: putter blocks, then getter takes the item via `get_nowait` \u2014 that resolves the putter. The putter's `set_result` is called, but the putter coroutine hasn't resumed yet (it's scheduled). So `_putters` is empty after `get_nowait`.\n\nWhat if the getter is a coroutine that hasn't run yet? E.g.:\n\n```python\nasync def main():\n    q = Queue(maxsize=1)\n    q.put_nowait(\"a\")  # queue full, unfinished_tasks=1\n    putter = q.put(\"b\")  # blocks, _putters=[f]\n    # Now check repr: putters[1] should appear\n    ...\n```\n\nThen to also have a getter waiting at the same time... The getter would immediately consume. Unless the getter has a timeout? No, timeout doesn't prevent immediate consumption.\n\nOK here's another thought: what if we manually manipulate? For a unit test aiming at coverage, directly appending a dummy future to `q._getters` is legitimate-ish but ugly. Better to keep tests using public API across multiple tests.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: can both be waiting with the actual implementation? Let me look at `put` with timeout and `get` with timeout... \n\nActually here's a scenario: \n1. `q = Queue(maxsize=1)`\n2. `putter = q.put(\"a\")` \u2014 queue empty, put succeeds immediately (not full). No.\n   \nWith maxsize=0 (unbounded), putters never wait.\n\nHmm, what about this: getter waiting, then `put_nowait` hands item to getter. Then queue is empty again. Then `putter = q.put(\"b\")` \u2014 queue empty, not full, put succeeds immediately. No putter waiting.\n\nWhat about: putter waiting (queue full), then `get_nowait()` \u2014 pops putter, returns item, queue now empty. Then `getter = q.get()` \u2014 queue empty, getter waits. But putter already resolved.\n\nSo indeed, with the public API, at any moment at most one of getters/putters is non-empty... Actually wait, is that true? Let me think about `_consume_expired` and timeouts.\n\nConsider:\n1. `q = Queue(maxsize=1)`\n2. `q.put_nowait(\"a\")` \u2014 full.\n3. `putter = q.put(\"b\", timeout=...)` \u2014 blocks.\n4. `getter = q.get()` \u2014 pops putter, gets \"a\", putter resolved.\n\nStill not both.\n\nWhat about a getter with a timeout that expired but hasn't been consumed? `_consume_expired` is called at the start of `put_nowait`, `get_nowait`, `put`, `get`. Hmm.\n\nActually, here's a real scenario where both can be waiting \u2014 I recall tornado's own tests have something like this. Let me think... In tornado's queues_test, there's a test with `repr`:\n\n```python\ndef test_repr(self):\n    q = Queue()\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me think about the actual tornado test suite. There is `test_format` maybe? Actually I don't think tornado tests `_format` directly.\n\nOK, regardless \u2014 the simplest robust approach: multiple tests, each covering different branches. But actually, can I get both getters and putters in one repr? Let me think harder.\n\nThe key insight: `put` when queue is full adds to `_putters`. `get` when queue is empty adds to `_getters`. The queue can't be both empty and full... unless maxsize handling... no.\n\nBUT: consider this sequence with direct future manipulation avoided:\n\n1. `q = Queue(maxsize=1)`\n2. `getter = q.get()` \u2014 getter waits (queue empty). `_getters = [g]`.\n3. `q.put_nowait(\"a\")` \u2014 since `_getters` non-empty, item handed to getter: `__put_internal(item)` then `getter.set_result(self._get())`. So `_unfinished_tasks = 1`, queue empty, `_getters` empty.\n\nHmm what if instead of `put_nowait`, we call `put` (async) \u2014 same logic.\n\nWhat if the getter future is resolved but the getter coroutine hasn't been awaited... The future is in `_getters`? No \u2014 `get_nowait`/`put_nowait` pop the getter from `_getters` before setting result. So `_getters` is empty.\n\nOK so truly, with public API, we can't have both non-empty simultaneously... \n\nWait, actually! What about this: a getter waits with a timeout. The timeout expires. `_consume_expired` removes expired getters... but only when put/get is called. So:\n\n1. `q = Queue(maxsize=1)`\n2. `getter = q.get(timeout=...)` \u2014 waits.\n3. Timeout expires (getter future gets exception set via `on_eof`... actually via `_consume_expired`? No \u2014 timeouts are handled by the IOLoop calling... let me recall.\n\nIn tornado's Queue:\n\n```python\ndef get(self, timeout=None):\n    ...\n    getter = futures.Future()\n    self._getters.append(getter)\n    if timeout is not None:\n        def on_timeout():\n            getter.set_exception(gen.TimeoutError())\n            self._consume_expired()\n        io_loop = ioloop.IOLoop.current()\n        timeout_handle = io_loop.add_timeout(timeout, on_timeout)\n        getter.add_done_callback(lambda f: io_loop.remove_timeout(timeout_handle))\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, roughly. So when timeout fires, the getter future gets an exception and `_consume_expired` removes it from `_getters`.\n\nOK so no way to have both. Fine \u2014 I'll write separate tests, or one test that checks repr at multiple points. Actually, one test function can do all of this sequentially with repr checks at each stage! That's cleaner:\n\n```python\nasync def test_format(self):\n    q = Queue(maxsize=1)\n    # Empty queue: only maxsize\n    assert repr(q) == \"<Queue maxsize=1>\"  # need to check exact format\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, what does `__repr__` look like? Let me check. `__repr__` is probably:\n\n```python\ndef __repr__(self):\n    return \"<%s %s>\" % (self.__class__.__name__, self._format())\n```\n\nLet me get info on `__repr__` and `__str__` to be sure. Also `put_nowait`, `put`, `get`, `task_done`, `_unfinished_tasks` behavior.\n\nActually, I should call get_info on `__repr__`, `__str__`, `put_nowait`, `put`, `get`, `task_done` to write accurate assertions.\n\nLet me also think about how to run async tests: `pytest-asyncio` with `@pytest.mark.asyncio`? Or use tornado's own testing? The safest for a generic pytest environment: use `tornado.testing.AsyncTestCase`? But that's unittest-style. Alternatively, run the event loop manually in a sync test:\n\n```python\ndef test_x(self):\n    async def main():\n        ...\n    IOLoop(...).run_sync(main)  # or asyncio.run\n```\n\nUsing `asyncio.run(main())` is simplest and doesn't depend on pytest-asyncio. But tornado's Queue uses `IOLoop.current()` for timeouts; without timeouts, `asyncio.run` works fine since Queue uses asyncio futures in modern tornado (tornado 6 uses `asyncio.Future` via `tornado.concurrent.Future`... actually tornado 6's futures are asyncio futures when the asyncio loop is running). `asyncio.run` sets up the loop, so `IOLoop.current()` works within it (tornado 6 integrates with asyncio).\n\nHmm, but to be safe and idiomatic, I could use `tornado.testing.gen_test` with `AsyncTestCase`. But the instructions say pytest test functions. `AsyncTestCase` is unittest-style. pytest can run unittest-style tests, but \"pytest test functions\" suggests plain functions.\n\nI'll use plain pytest functions with `asyncio.run(...)` inside, or check if pytest-asyncio is available... Since I can't be sure, `asyncio.run` is safest. Actually, tornado's own test suite uses `pytest` with `@pytest.mark.asyncio`? No \u2014 tornado uses its own `AsyncTestCase` with pytest. Hmm, tornado 6.2+ tests use `unittest` style run under pytest.\n\nI'll go with plain functions calling `asyncio.run()`. But wait \u2014 `Queue.get()` without timeout doesn't need IOLoop; it uses futures. `put` with timeout uses `IOLoop.current()` \u2014 within asyncio.run, `IOLoop.current()` returns an IOLoop wrapping the running asyncio loop (tornado 6 does this via `AsyncIOLoop`... actually `IOLoop.current()` inside a running asyncio loop returns the loop's IOLoop if configured). In tornado 6, `IOLoop.current()` will find the current asyncio loop and return an IOLoop instance associated with it. Yes, tornado 6.x: \"IOLoop.current() ... if there is a current asyncio loop, return the IOLoop for it\". So timeouts work under asyncio.run.\n\nTo avoid timeout complexity, I'll avoid timeouts entirely.\n\nNow, plan the test(s):\n\nTest A: `test_queue_format_empty` \u2014 empty queue, repr/str contains only \"maxsize=0\", no \"queue=\", no \"getters[\", no \"putters[\", no \"tasks=\". Covers line 337 and false branches.\n\nTest B: `test_queue_format_with_items_and_tasks` \u2014 put items without task_done: covers 338-339 (queue=...) and 344-345 (tasks=...).\n\nTest C: `test_queue_format_getter_waiting` \u2014 start `q.get()` on empty queue, check repr contains \"getters[1]\". Then clean up: put an item to resolve the getter, and consume it / or cancel. Need to make sure the pending future doesn't cause \"Future exception was never retrieved\" warnings. If we put an item, the getter future gets a result \u2014 fine. Then we should retrieve it: `await q.get()`? Wait, the getter future was created by `q.get()`; if we `put_nowait`, the future gets result set. The coroutine/future `getter` \u2014 we should await it or add done callback to avoid warnings. Actually `Future.set_result` on a future that's never awaited logs \"Future exception was never retrieved\" only for exceptions. For results, no warning. But to be clean, await it.\n\nHmm, but `q.get()` returns a Future (in tornado 6, `get` returns a Future directly, not a coroutine \u2014 actually it returns `Awaitable`; the implementation returns a Future). Let me check: \n\n```python\ndef get(self, timeout=None):\n    ...\n    return getter\n```\n\nYes, `get` returns a Future. So `getter = q.get()` creates the future and appends to `_getters` immediately (synchronously). \n\nSo in an async test:\n```python\nq = Queue(maxsize=1)\ngetter = q.get()  # blocks, _getters has 1\nassert \"getters[1]\" in repr(q)\nq.put_nowait(\"x\")  # resolves getter\nassert await getter == \"x\"\n```\n\nWait \u2014 does `put_nowait` hand the item to the getter? Yes: `if self._getters: ... getter.set_result(self._get())`. And `self._get()` pops from `_queue` after `__put_internal(item)` puts it. So getter gets \"x\". \n\nTest D: `test_queue_format_putter_waiting` \u2014 \n```python\nq = Queue(maxsize=1)\nq.put_nowait(\"a\")  # full\nputter = q.put(\"b\")  # blocks, _putters has 1\nassert \"putters[1]\" in repr(q)\nassert await q.get() == \"a\"  # resolves putter\nawait putter  # put completes\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `q.put(\"b\")` \u2014 is `put` a coroutine or does it return a Future? In tornado 6: `def put(self, item, timeout=None) -> Future[None]:` \u2014 it returns a Future. Let me verify with get_info.\n\nAlso note: `put` when not full does `__put_internal(item)` and sets its own future result immediately? Let me recall:\n\n```python\ndef put(self, item, timeout=None):\n    future = futures.Future()  # or asyncio Future\n    ...\n    if not self.full():\n        ...\n        self.__put_internal(item)\n        future.set_result(None)\n    else:\n        self._putters.append((item, future))\n        ...\n    return future\n```\n\nHmm, roughly. Actually in tornado 6:\n\n```python\ndef put(self, item, timeout=None):\n    future = _awaitable_to_future?  \n```\n\nLet me just get_info on `put`, `put_nowait`, `get`, `get_nowait`, `task_done`, `__repr__`, `__str__`, `_consume_expired`.\n\nAlso for the \"tasks=\" branch: `_unfinished_tasks` is incremented in `__put_internal`. So after `put_nowait(\"a\")`, `_unfinished_tasks == 1`. repr should contain \"tasks=1\".\n\nNow, can I combine C and D into one test to also check both branches? They can't coexist, but sequential checks in one test work. However, separate tests are clearer. The instruction: \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\". Multiple functions are fine.\n\nAlso need to handle cleanup / state pollution: each test creates its own Queue, so no global state. But pending futures/coroutines could leak warnings. I'll make sure every started future is resolved and awaited.\n\nOne concern: in test C, after `q.put_nowait(\"x\")`, the getter future is resolved. `await getter` retrieves it. Good.\n\nIn test D: after `await q.get()` (which pops putter and sets its result), `await putter` completes. Good.\n\nAlso, `q.get()` returns a future; `await q.get()` works.\n\nNow about `__repr__` format \u2014 I need the exact string. Let me get_info on `__repr__` and `__str__`.\n\nAlso, is `_format` reachable via `repr(q)`? `__repr__` calls `self._format()`. Let me confirm.\n\nLet me also double check `Queue._unfinished_tasks` semantics: `task_done` decrements. In test B, I'll put 2 items and not call task_done, so \"tasks=2\" appears.\n\nAlso the `getattr(self, \"_queue\", None)` check: `_queue` is a class attribute set to None, and `_init` sets `self._queue = collections.deque()`. For an instance, `getattr(self, \"_queue\", None)` returns the deque (empty deque is falsy). So the false branch is covered by any normal instance. The `getattr` default (attribute missing) would only matter for subclasses that don't call `_init`... e.g., a subclass overriding `_init` to not set `_queue`. Hmm, branch coverage of line 338: the `if` true/false branches. False branch covered by empty queue. True branch covered by non-empty queue. Good.\n\nNow, exact repr format. Let me fetch info.\n\nAlso \u2014 potential issue: `Queue` in tornado 6.5 might use `asyncio.Queue`-like internals? No, tornado has its own.\n\nLet me also consider: should tests use `pytest.mark.asyncio`? If pytest-asyncio isn't installed, the mark would error (strict mode) or be ignored (auto mode). Safer: plain sync test functions that call `asyncio.run(...)` on an inner async main. That works everywhere with tornado 6 (which requires asyncio anyway).\n\nBut wait \u2014 tornado's `Queue.get()`/`put()` return futures tied to the current event loop. Inside `asyncio.run`, that's fine.\n\nEdge: `IOLoop.current()` usage in `put`/`get` with timeout \u2014 we avoid timeouts.\n\nLet me now fetch the needed info.",
