hon's deque repr is `deque(['a', 'b'])`. Yes.\n\nNow, imports:\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom tornado.queues import Queue\n```\n\nDo I need pytest imports? Not really, but maybe `pytest.mark.asyncio`? I'll avoid it. Plain functions.\n\nShould I guard against pytest-asyncio auto mode interfering? Plain sync functions are fine.\n\nLet me also think about whether `q.get()` in test 3 could raise if... no.\n\nOne more: in test 3, after `q.put_nowait(\"x\")`, there's an assert in `put_nowait`: `assert self.empty(), 'queue non-empty, why are getters waiting?'` \u2014 queue is empty (getter waiting means empty), so fine.\n\nIn test 4, `await q.get()` \u2192 `get_nowait` \u2192 `assert self.full(), 'queue empty, why are putters waiting?'` \u2014 queue has \"a\", maxsize=1 \u2192 full. Fine.\n\nEdge: `Queue(maxsize=1)`, `put_nowait(\"a\")` \u2192 `full()` returns `qsize == maxsize` \u2192 1 == 1 \u2192 True. Good.\n\nNow write the final file. I'll structure:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for tornado.queues.Queue._format (lines 337-346).\"\"\"\n\nimport asyncio\n\nfrom tornado.queues import Queue\n\n\ndef test_str_empty_queue_covers_maxsize_only():\n    q = Queue(maxsize=3)\n    s = str(q)\n    assert s == \"<Queue maxsize=3>\"\n    assert \"queue=\" not in s\n    assert \"getters[\" not in s\n    assert \"putters[\" not in s\n    assert \"tasks=\" not in s\n    r = repr(q)\n    assert r.startswith(\"<Queue at 0x\")\n    assert r.endswith(\" maxsize=3>\")\n```\n\nHmm, repr format: `'<%s at %s %s>' % (type(self).__name__, hex(id(self)), self._format())` \u2192 `<Queue at 0x... maxsize=3>`. So `r == \"<Queue at %s maxsize=3>\" % hex(id(q))`. I can assert that exactly:\n\n```python\nassert r == \"<Queue at %s maxsize=3>\" % hex(id(q))\n```\n\nThat's exact and correct.\n\n```python\ndef test_str_with_items_and_unfinished_tasks():\n    q = Queue(maxsize=0)\n    q.put_nowait(\"a\")\n    q.put_nowait(\"b\")\n    assert str(q) == \"<Queue maxsize=0 queue=deque(['a', 'b']) tasks=2>\"\n    # task_done clears the tasks part\n    q.task_done()\n    assert str(q) == \"<Queue maxsize=0 queue=deque(['a', 'b']) tasks=1>\"\n    q.task_done()\n    assert str(q) == \"<Queue maxsize=0 queue=deque(['a', 'b'])>\"\n```\n\nWait \u2014 after task_done twice, `_unfinished_tasks == 0`, so no \"tasks=\" part. Good. This covers both true and false branches of line 344 within one test, plus 338-339 true.\n\nHmm, but the false branch of 338 (empty `_queue`) \u2014 covered in test 1. And true branch here. Good.\n\n```python\ndef test_str_with_waiting_getter():\n    async def main():\n        q = Queue(maxsize=1)\n        getter = q.get()  # empty queue -> getter parks in _getters\n        assert str(q) == \"<Queue maxsize=1 getters[1]>\"\n        q.put_nowait(\"x\")  # item handed straight to the waiting getter\n        assert await getter == \"x\"\n        assert str(q) == \"<Queue maxsize=1>\"\n    asyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_str_with_waiting_putter():\n    async def main():\n        q = Queue(maxsize=1)\n        q.put_nowait(\"a\")  # fills the queue, registers an unfinished task\n        putter = q.put(\"b\")  # queue full -> putter parks in _putters\n        assert str(q) == \"<Queue maxsize=1 queue=deque(['a']) putters[1] tasks=1>\"\n        assert await q.get() == \"a\"  # taking an item resolves the putter\n        await putter\n        assert str(q) == \"<Queue maxsize=1 queue=deque(['b']) tasks=2>\"\n    asyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nWait, in the last assertion: after `await q.get()`, queue contains \"b\" (put by the resolved putter via `__put_internal`? No wait). Let me re-trace: `put(\"b\")` on full queue \u2192 `self._putters.append((\"b\", future))`. The item \"b\" is NOT in `_queue` yet. Then `get_nowait` \u2192 `_putters` non-empty \u2192 pops `(\"b\", putter)`, `self._get()` pops \"a\" from `_queue`, `putter.set_result(None)`, returns \"a\". So `_queue` is now EMPTY, not `deque(['b'])`! The putter's item \"b\" was never added to the queue \u2014 the putter future just resolves, and the putter coroutine (if any) resumes knowing its put completed. Since we called `q.put(\"b\")` directly (not via a coroutine that would re-put), \"b\" is simply gone.\n\nSo after `await q.get()`: `_queue` is empty, `_unfinished_tasks` is 1 (only \"a\" was put via `__put_internal`; the parked putter's item never went through `__put_internal`). Let me verify: `put` on QueueFull just appends to `_putters` \u2014 no `__put_internal`. So `_unfinished_tasks` stays 1. After `get_nowait` pops the putter: `_get()` pops \"a\" \u2014 does `_get` decrement unfinished tasks? No, `task_done` does. So `_unfinished_tasks` remains 1.\n\nSo `str(q)` after `await q.get()` \u2192 `\"<Queue maxsize=1 tasks=1>\"`. Hmm wait, queue empty \u2192 no \"queue=\" part. So `\"<Queue maxsize=1 tasks=1>\"`.\n\nLet me redo:\n- `q.put_nowait(\"a\")`: `_queue = deque(['a'])`, `_unfinished_tasks = 1`.\n- `putter = q.put(\"b\")`: QueueFull \u2192 `_putters = [((\"b\", future))]`. Queue unchanged.\n- `str(q)`: maxsize=1, queue=deque(['a']), putters[1], tasks=1 \u2192 `\"<Queue maxsize=1 queue=deque(['a']) putters[1] tasks=1>\"`. \u2713.\n- `await q.get()`: get_nowait \u2192 `_putters` non-empty \u2192 assert full \u2713 \u2192 pop (\"b\", putter) \u2192 `_get()` pops \"a\" \u2192 `_queue` empty \u2192 putter.set_result(None) \u2192 return \"a\". So `await q.get()` == \"a\" \u2713.\n- `await putter` \u2192 None \u2713.\n- `str(q)` now: `_queue` empty, `_putters` empty, `_unfinished_tasks` == 1 \u2192 `\"<Queue maxsize=1 tasks=1>\"`.\n\nGood, I'll assert that. And to clean up fully, call `q.task_done()` \u2192 tasks part disappears: `\"<Queue maxsize=1>\"`. Nice postcondition.\n\nAlso in test 3 (getter): after `put_nowait(\"x\")` with getter waiting: `__put_internal(\"x\")` \u2192 `_queue = deque(['x'])`, `_unfinished_tasks = 1`; then `self._get()` pops \"x\" \u2192 `_queue` empty; getter gets \"x\". So after: queue empty, tasks=1. So `str(q)` == `\"<Queue maxsize=1 tasks=1>\"`, not `\"<Queue maxsize=1>\"`. Let me fix that assertion!\n\nRe-trace test 3:\n- `q = Queue(maxsize=1)`, empty.\n- `getter = q.get()`: QueueEmpty \u2192 `_getters = [future]`. `str(q)` \u2192 `\"<Queue maxsize=1 getters[1]>\"` \u2713.\n- `q.put_nowait(\"x\")`: `_getters` non-empty \u2192 assert empty \u2713 \u2192 pop getter \u2192 `__put_internal(\"x\")` (unfinished_tasks=1, queue=['x']) \u2192 `future_set_result_unless_cancelled(getter, self._get())` \u2192 `_get()` pops \"x\" \u2192 queue empty. getter resolves with \"x\".\n- `await getter` == \"x\" \u2713.\n- `str(q)` \u2192 `\"<Queue maxsize=1 tasks=1>\"` (queue empty, no getters, tasks=1).\n\nThen cleanup: `q.task_done()` \u2192 `\"<Queue maxsize=1>\"`. \n\nNow, is `future_set_result_unless_cancelled` synchronous? Yes, it sets the result immediately. So `await getter` right after works.\n\nAlso note: in test 3, `q.get()` returns a Future \u2014 awaiting it later is fine.\n\nNow, one concern about `asyncio.run` and tornado's `Future`: `tornado.concurrent.Future` \u2014 in tornado 6, it's `asyncio.Future` if available. `await` on asyncio.Future inside asyncio.run works. \u2713.\n\nAnother concern: `asyncio.run(main())` \u2014 inside, `Queue` creation doesn't need a loop. `q.get()` creates `Future()` \u2014 `asyncio.Future()` requires a running loop? `asyncio.Future()` uses `get_event_loop` internally... In Python 3.10+, `asyncio.Future()` without a running loop raises DeprecationWarning but still works via get_event_loop? Actually `asyncio.Future.__init__` calls `events.get_event_loop()` if loop is None \u2014 inside `asyncio.run`, there IS a running loop, so fine. And we call it inside the coroutine anyway (running loop guaranteed). \u2713.\n\nFor the sync tests (test 1, test 2), no futures are created \u2014 `put_nowait` doesn't create futures. \u2713. `task_done` doesn't. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 should I also add a test that exercises `_format` when `_queue` attribute is missing entirely (the `getattr` default)? Line 338's `getattr(self, \"_queue\", None)` \u2014 the default only matters if `_queue` doesn't exist. A subclass that overrides `_init` to not set `_queue`... Actually `_queue = None` is a class attribute, so `getattr` always finds it on instances of Queue. The default only kicks in for exotic subclasses. Branch coverage-wise, the `if` statement's branches are covered by empty/non-empty queue. I could add a subclass test for completeness:\n\n```python\ndef test_str_queue_attribute_missing():\n    class NoInitQueue(Queue):\n        def _init(self):\n            pass  # never sets self._queue\n    q = NoInitQueue(maxsize=2)\n    assert str(q) == \"<NoInitQueue maxsize=2>\"\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `Queue.__init__` calls `self._init()`. If `_init` doesn't set `_queue`, then `getattr(self, \"_queue\", None)` \u2014 but `_queue = None` is a CLASS attribute of Queue! So `getattr(self, \"_queue\", None)` returns None (class attr), not the default. So the getattr default is truly unreachable for Queue subclasses unless they delete the class attr too. E.g.:\n\n```python\nclass NoQueueQueue(Queue):\n    _queue = None  # still present...\n```\n\nTo trigger the getattr default, you'd need `del` or a subclass without the attribute \u2014 but it's defined on Queue itself, so any subclass inherits it. Only `object`-level absence would trigger default. Not worth it; the `if` branch coverage is what matters (338's condition true/false). I'll skip that exotic case \u2014 actually, hmm, \"execute all missing lines and branches\". The branches of line 338 are: condition true \u2192 line 339; condition false \u2192 skip to 340. Both covered. The `getattr` default value is not a branch. OK, skip.\n\nLet me also double check `Queue(maxsize=3)` \u2014 `__init__` validates maxsize. Fine.\n\nAlso, should I use `pytest` fixture for cleanup? The queues are local; asyncio.run closes loops. No global state. I think that's clean. The instruction mentions monkeypatch/pytest-mock \"if appropriate\" \u2014 not needed here since no global state is touched.\n\nOne more consideration: tornado version compatibility. In older tornado (5.x), `Queue` used `gen` and `Future` differently, and `asyncio.run` might not integrate. But tornado 6 is standard now. Also `future_set_result_unless_cancelled` exists in tornado 6.0+. The code shown uses `future_set_result_unless_cancelled`, so it's tornado \u2265 6.0. Good.\n\nAlso, in tornado 6.x, is `Queue.get()` returning a Future that can be awaited multiple times? We await once. Fine.\n\nLet me also think: do I want to combine tests 3 and 4 into one async test? Separate is fine and clearer. But maybe combine to reduce loop creation? Not necessary.\n\nAlso, maybe add a test covering `_format` via `repr` with everything... can't have getters+putters together. Fine.\n\nLet me also double-check the exact repr of deque with one string element: `deque(['a'])`. \u2713.\n\nAnd `hex(id(q))` format: e.g. `0x7f...`. `repr` \u2192 `<Queue at 0x7f... maxsize=3>`. My assertion `r == \"<Queue at %s maxsize=3>\" % hex(id(q))` \u2014 careful: `id(q)` must be taken while q is alive; it is. \u2713.\n\nNow write the final file with docstrings and comments. Also include `import pytest`? Not needed. Maybe use `pytest.raises`? Not needed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider using `asyncio.run` vs `tornado.ioloop.IOLoop.run_sync`. With asyncio.run, tornado's IOLoop isn't explicitly started, but Queue doesn't need IOLoop without timeouts. `Future()` = asyncio.Future works under asyncio.run. \u2713. I'll use asyncio.run for simplicity and no tornado IOLoop dependency.\n\nHmm, one thing: tornado 6.2+ `tornado.concurrent.Future` is `asyncio.Future`. In tornado 6.0/6.1, `tornado.concurrent.Future` was a custom class that was asyncio-compatible when a loop was running... Actually tornado 6.0's Future: \"If the asyncio loop is running, tornado.concurrent.Future is asyncio.Future\". There was `tornado.concurrent.Future` implemented to delegate